---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import ImageFigure from '../components/ImageFigure.astro';
import PipelineDiagram from '../components/PipelineDiagram.astro';
import OracleDiagram from '../components/OracleDiagram.astro';
import chase from '../assets/screenshots/gallery_f2004_spa_chase.webp';
import cockpit from '../assets/screenshots/gallery_f2004_cockpit.webp';
import trackside from '../assets/screenshots/gallery_f2004_spa_trackside.webp';
import { LINKS } from '../lib/site';
import { shareImage } from '../lib/og';

const ogImage = await shareImage(trackside, 'The Ferrari F2004 passing the grandstand at Spa, drawn by rustyAC.');

const description =
  'How rustyAC rebuilds Assetto Corsa in Rust and proves it: decompile acs.exe with its debug symbols, map each system, port it one to one, then compare every bit against the original game’s own code.';

const TOC = [
  { id: 'decompile', label: '1. Decompile' },
  { id: 'map', label: '2. Map' },
  { id: 'port', label: '3. Port' },
  { id: 'prove', label: '4. Prove' },
  { id: 'maths', label: 'The maths library' },
  { id: 'fma3', label: 'The FMA3 finding' },
  { id: 'bit-exact', label: 'What "bit-exact" means here' },
];

const MAP = `${LINKS.github}/blob/master/docs/map/`;
const PORT = `${LINKS.github}/blob/master/docs/port/`;
const ORACLE = `${LINKS.github}/blob/master/docs/oracle/`;

// One row per proof. The source of each number is the report in the last field.
const PROOFS: { what: string; count: string; result: string; report: string }[] = [
  // docs/port/sctm.md, section 1
  { what: 'The tyre formula', count: '329,734 rows, seven numbers each', result: 'zero differences', report: 'sctm.md' },
  // docs/port/tyre_step.md, section 1
  { what: 'A whole tyre', count: '97,800 steps', result: 'the same in every step', report: 'tyre_step.md' },
  // docs/port/ode_stage1.md, section 1
  { what: 'The rigid-body solver', count: '1,996,043 steps', result: 'identical', report: 'ode_stage1.md' },
  // docs/port/whole_car.md, section 1 and the results table
  { what: 'A whole car', count: '49 drives, 228,184 steps', result: 'identical', report: 'whole_car.md' },
  // docs/port/tracks.md, section 1
  { what: 'Every track', count: '8,800,000 ray casts; 89 drives, 2,752,168 steps', result: 'zero differences', report: 'tracks.md' },
  // docs/port/audio.md, section 1
  { what: 'Sound', count: '18 drives, 16,704,338 FMOD calls', result: 'every argument bit-equal', report: 'audio.md' },
  // docs/port/renderer_car.md, summary
  { what: 'The car, drawn', count: '27 sequences, 2,353 frames', result: 'same commands, same pixels', report: 'renderer_car.md' },
  // docs/port/web.md, section 3.2
  { what: 'The browser build', count: '160 drives, 1,787,777 steps', result: 'equal to the desktop', report: 'web.md' },
];
---

<Page
  title="How it works"
  intro="rustyAC is not written to feel like Assetto Corsa. It is written to compute the same numbers, and then made to prove it."
  description={description}
  toc={TOC}
  ogImage={ogImage}
>
  <p>
    There are four steps, and every part of the game goes through all of them: decompile the original, map what it
    does, port it to Rust one to one, and prove the port by running it next to the original's own code until every
    bit matches.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram />

  <h2 id="decompile">1. Decompile</h2>
  <!-- rustyAC docs/map/physics_engine.md line 3 (version, date); docs/map/yebis.md line 3 (size); docs/map/tyre.md lines 3-4 (Ghidra 12.1.2, the PDB's names); brainstorm.md section 0 (acs.pdb, 66 MB) -->
  <p>
    Assetto Corsa is one native program, <code>acs.exe</code> (game version 1.16.4, built on 2018-01-10,
    22,890,776 bytes). It ships with its debug symbols, <code>acs.pdb</code>, a 66 MB file that holds the real names
    of the game's functions and data. Loaded into Ghidra 12 together, the decompiler shows
    <code>Tyre::addTyreForcesV10</code> where it would otherwise show an address.
  </p>
  <!-- docs/map/car.md section 5.1 (7,453 functions); docs/map/physics_engine.md lines 43-48 (ODE 0.13.1, 609 functions) -->
  <p>
    All 7,453 hand-written functions of the game were decompiled. The symbols also show what the game is built on:
    its rigid-body physics is ODE 0.13.1, an open-source library, compiled into the program (609 functions of it).
  </p>

  <h2 id="map">2. Map</h2>
  <!-- docs/map/car.md, index and section 5.1 item 3 -->
  <p>
    Before any Rust is written, each system gets a written report: what its functions do, in what order, with which
    constants, and which file each number comes from. There are sixteen of these maps, from the tyre to the
    force-feedback number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each map was then attacked by a second, independent reviewer: 658 claims were checked against the code, and 54
    were found wrong and fixed. Every address in every document is checked by a script.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few to read:
    <a href={`${MAP}car.md`} rel="noopener">the whole car</a>,
    <a href={`${MAP}car_step.md`} rel="noopener">one physics step</a>,
    <a href={`${MAP}tyre.md`} rel="noopener">the tyre</a>,
    <a href={`${MAP}physics_engine.md`} rel="noopener">the physics engine</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="port">3. Port</h2>
  <!-- docs/port/sctm.md section 4 (transcribed from the disassembly; the regrouped products); docs/port/awd_ers.md lines 77-79 -->
  <p>
    The port is Rust, one to one: the same functions, the same fields, the same order of operations. The rule that
    makes it work is to <strong>transcribe from the disassembly, not from the decompiler's pseudo-C</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pseudo-C is right about <em>what</em> is computed, but it regroups arithmetic. In the tyre formula it shows
    <code>(cos · speed) · slipRatio</code> where the real code computes <code>cos · (speed · slipRatio)</code>. With
    floating-point numbers those two can differ in the last bit, and a last bit is the whole point.
  </p>
  <!-- docs/port/ode_stage1.md, the table of differences (rows 1 and 2) and section 1 -->
  <p>
    The same goes for source code, where there is any. The physics library's source is public, but a literal port
    of it matched the game on only 54 % of the steps of a car: the compiler had turned some sums into packed
    four-lane additions, which add up in a different order. So the game's machine code was followed, not the source
    text.
  </p>

  <ImageFigure
    src={chase}
    alt="The Ferrari F2004 at speed at Spa, seen from the chase camera in rustyAC"
    caption="The F2004 at Spa, 175 km/h, drawn by rustyAC with Assetto Corsa's own shaders."
  />

  <h2 id="prove">4. Prove</h2>
  <!-- docs/map/tyre_oracle.md, part C "How it calls AC's code"; docs/oracle/car_oracle.md lines 75-76 -->
  <p>
    The test tool is called <strong>the oracle</strong>. It loads the real <code>acs.exe</code> into its own
    process as a plain block of code. The game never starts: its entry point and start-up code never run. The
    oracle then calls the game's own functions directly, by the addresses from <code>acs.pdb</code>, and asks the
    Rust port the same question. The two answers are compared as bit patterns, not as rounded numbers.
  </p>

  <OracleDiagram />

  <p>
    It refuses to run against any other build of the game than the one that was decompiled. And the comparison is
    checked too: deliberate small faults were put into the Rust car, one at a time (24 in the whole car), and each
    one was caught.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>What</th><th>How much</th><th>Result</th><th>Report</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          PROOFS.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.what}</td>
              <td class="mono">{row.count}</td>
              <td>{row.result}</td>
              <td>
                <a href={`${PORT}${row.report}`} rel="noopener">
                  read
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>The car oracle</h3>
  <!-- docs/oracle/car_oracle.md, summary (lines 36-49) and section 2.1 -->
  <p>
    For a whole car, the oracle builds the game's real car, a Ferrari F2004 with all its parts, by calling the game's
    own constructors. It gives it a flat road, a scripted driver and a clock that only moves when told to. Twelve
    drives were recorded this way, 70,673 steps in all; each was run twice from scratch, and both runs give the same
    checksum over every byte. Those recordings are what the Rust car is held against.
    <a href={`${ORACLE}car_oracle.md`} rel="noopener">The car oracle's report</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>The render oracle</h3>
  <!-- docs/port/renderer_core.md sections 1 and 3; docs/port/postfx.md lines 56-59 -->
  <p>
    The renderer is proven the same way. The game's own renderer and the port each draw the same frame on WARP,
    Windows' software graphics card, which gives the same pixels on every run. Two things are compared: the list of
    Direct3D 11 commands, as text, and the finished picture, byte by byte. On the first 21 frames the two command
    lists were identical line for line (up to 5,943 commands a frame) and the pictures identical in every byte.
    Later tasks added whole sequences, and a real graphics card: on a Radeon the port matches the game's renderer
    with 1, 2, 4 and 8 anti-aliasing samples.
  </p>

  <ImageFigure
    src={cockpit}
    alt="The cockpit of the Ferrari F2004 at Spa in rustyAC, with the steering wheel display lit"
    caption="The F2004's cockpit: the dials, the display and the mirrors are the game's own, drawn by the port."
  />

  <h3>The audio oracle</h3>
  <!-- docs/port/audio.md sections 1, 4 and 5.2 -->
  <p>
    Sound is Assetto Corsa's own: rustyAC loads the game's FMOD libraries and sound banks from your install. The
    oracle logs every call into FMOD from the game and from the port. On 18 recorded drives the port makes exactly
    the calls the game makes: 16,704,338 of them, every argument bit-equal. The sound itself is written to WAV
    files without a sound card, faster than real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those WAV files are not identical, and that is FMOD: it mixes with random numbers of its own, so two runs of the
    game's own code never gave the same file either. The port's sound is held to the game's own run-to-run
    difference instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="maths">The maths library</h2>
  <!-- docs/port/web.md section 2.1 (the imports), section 1 and the proof table (lines 62-68, 119-160) -->
  <p>
    The game does not compute its own sines and powers. It calls <code>sinf</code>, <code>cosf</code>,
    <code>powf</code> and a few more from <code>MSVCR120.dll</code>, the Visual C++ 2013 runtime. A different
    <code>sinf</code> would be a different game, so on the desktop rustyAC calls that same DLL.
  </p>
  <p>
    A browser cannot load a DLL. So the ten functions were read out of the DLL's machine code and written again in
    Rust, instruction by instruction, and then proven:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      for each of the seven one-argument functions, <strong>every one of the 4,294,967,296 possible inputs</strong>
      gives the same bits as the DLL
    </li>
    <li>
      the two-argument functions (<code>powf</code>, <code>atan2f</code>) have too many input pairs to try them
      all, so they were tried on 59 billion more inputs: not one difference
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That copy is what lets the <a href="/play">browser preview</a> stay exact. It is also the desktop's fallback
    when the DLL is not installed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fma3">The FMA3 finding</h2>
  <!-- docs/port/web.md section 1 item 2 (lines 68-72), section 2.1 (lines 96-101) and the fma3-off table (lines 162-176) -->
  <p>
    Reading the DLL turned up a surprise about the game itself. The DLL has two versions of six of these functions
    and picks one by the processor: one for CPUs with the FMA3 instructions (everything since about 2013), one for
    older ones. The two do not give the same results: 44,232 of the 4.29 billion <code>sinf</code> inputs differ,
    and 18,018 for <code>cosf</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    So <strong>Assetto Corsa is not bit-exact with itself</strong>: a drive recorded on a pre-2013 processor would
    not replay bit for bit on a modern one, in the real game either. rustyAC's Rust copy is the modern path, the
    one the game runs on any current processor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bit-exact">What "bit-exact" means here</h2>
  <!-- docs/port/sctm.md lines 30-31 (the definition); docs/port/web.md lines 99-101, 174-176, 306-310 -->
  <p>
    It means this and nothing more: <strong
      >for the same inputs, on the same processor path, the port's numbers have the same bits as the numbers from
      the game's own code.</strong
    > The game is plain Assetto Corsa 1.16.4, without Custom Shaders Patch.
  </p>
  <p>What it does not mean, and what is not proven yet:</p>
  <ul>
    <!-- docs/port/web.md section 8, items 1 and 2 -->
    <li>
      <strong>Older processors.</strong> The maths path of CPUs without FMA3 is not ported. One more instruction
      gives different last bits on Intel and AMD processors; the browser build computes what an Intel desktop
      computes.
    </li>
    <!-- docs/port/web.md lines 309-320 -->
    <li>
      <strong>The browser.</strong> The 160-drive proof runs the physics as WebAssembly outside a browser. In the
      browser itself the check is a 10,000-step test drive on three car and track pairs.
    </li>
    <!-- docs/port/audio.md section 5.2 -->
    <li><strong>The sound mix.</strong> The calls are identical; the mixed samples are not, because FMOD is random.</li>
    <!-- docs/port/postfx.md section 9; docs/port/renderer_core.md lines 120-123 -->
    <li>
      <strong>The picture.</strong> The game's post-processing filters are not ported yet. The pixel proofs are for
      the renderer without them.
    </li>
    <!-- docs/port/drivetrain.md section 6.3; docs/port/whole_car.md sections 3.4 and 6.3 -->
    <li>
      <strong>Code no test reached.</strong> A recorded drive only proves the branches it takes. The reports list
      the ones that were ported from the disassembly but never run against the game.
    </li>
    <!-- docs/port/tracks.md section 8; docs/port/several_cars.md -->
    <li><strong>What is not ported at all</strong>: AI drivers, race rules, menus. See <a href="/progress">progress</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    All of it is in the open: the <a href={LINKS.docs} rel="noopener">reports</a> say what was run, how to run it
    again, and what was left out.
  </p>
</Page>
